Q: Can I use unique_ptr with inherited classes? A: <blockquote>
<p>Can I use unique_ptr with inherited classes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes.<br />
<code>std::unique_ptr</code> supports using the pointer polymorphically, similarly to a raw pointer (and this is relevant for class members as well).<br />
For C++11, you can initialize the smart pointer from a <code>new B</code> or <code>new C</code>.</p>
<p>This is demonstrated below:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

struct A { 
    virtual ~A(){}   // virtual destructor is required !!!
    virtual void do_something() {} 
};

struct B : public A { 
    void do_something() override { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::do_something()\n&quot;; }
};

struct C : public A {
    void do_something() override { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C::do_something()\n&quot;; }
};

class Foo
{
public:
    void someFunction(bool cond)
    {
        if (cond) {
            // Create a B:
            a = std::unique_ptr&lt;A&gt;(new B);
        }
        else {
            // Create a C:
            a = std::unique_ptr&lt;A&gt;(new C);
        }
        // Invoke a virtual method:
        a-&gt;do_something();
    }
private:
    std::unique_ptr&lt;A&gt; a;
};

int main() {
    Foo foo;
    foo.someFunction(true);
    foo.someFunction(false);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>B::do_something()
C::do_something()
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/3e4jxWjE8" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 1</a>.</p>
<p>If you can use C++14, there's a better option to initialize the smart pointer with <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/memory/unique_ptr/make_unique" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::make_unique</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>a = std::make_unique&lt;B&gt;();
// or:
a = std::make_unique&lt;C&gt;();
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/8cG3jW363" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 2</a>.</p>
<p>Finally, as @j6t commented below, you can use <code>std::unique_ptr::reset()</code> to assign the smart pointer to own a new object of class <code>A</code> or any derived, e.g.:</p>
<pre><code>a.reset(new B)
</code></pre>

Q: How to create a query for displaying the lessons from the current category(course) LearnDash customization? A: <p>This code fetches <strong>published LearnDash lessons</strong> (<code>sfwd-lessons</code>) that belong to a <strong>specific Lesson Category</strong> and displays their titles in a simple loop.<br />
All values (<code>post type</code>, <code>taxonomy</code>, <code>term ID</code>) come from <strong>LearnDash + WordPress</strong> and can be verified from the <strong>Admin panel or database</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Code with Clear Comments</h2>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
// Query arguments for fetching LearnDash lessons
$args = array(

    // LearnDash lesson post type
    // Found in wp_posts table or LearnDash documentation
    'post_type' =&gt; 'sfwd-lessons',

    // Sort lessons by publish date
    'orderby' =&gt; 'post_date',

    // DESC = newest first, ASC = oldest first
    'order' =&gt; 'DESC',

    // -1 means fetch all lessons (no limit)
    'posts_per_page' =&gt; -1,

    // Only fetch published lessons
    'post_status' =&gt; 'publish',

    // Taxonomy query to filter lessons by category
    'tax_query' =&gt; array(
        array(
            // LearnDash Lesson Category taxonomy
            // Can be confirmed from Lessons → Categories URL
            'taxonomy' =&gt; 'ld_lesson_category',

            // The value used in 'terms' is a TERM ID
            'field' =&gt; 'id',

            // Lesson Category ID
            // Found by hovering over category in admin (tag_ID)
            'terms' =&gt; 26,
        ),
    ),
);

// Create custom query
$q = new WP_Query($args);
?&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;timeline&quot;&gt;
    &lt;?php if ($q-&gt;have_posts()) : ?&gt;

        &lt;?php while ($q-&gt;have_posts()) : $q-&gt;the_post(); ?&gt;

            &lt;!-- Output lesson title --&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;&lt;?php the_title(); ?&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;

        &lt;?php endwhile; ?&gt;

        &lt;?php
        // Reset post data after custom query
        wp_reset_postdata();
        ?&gt;

    &lt;?php else : ?&gt;

        &lt;!-- Shown if no lessons are found --&gt;
        &lt;?php _e('No Posts Sorry.'); ?&gt;

    &lt;?php endif; ?&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<hr />

Q: LangChain.js createHistoryAwareRetriever with Ollama embeddings throws invalid input type error A: <p>Use compatible Langchain packages.</p>
<p><code>@langchain/classic</code> introduces <strong>v1.x LangChain functionality</strong> into a <strong>v0.3.x project</strong>. This creates a dependency conflict:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>@langchain/classic</strong> v1.0.x requires <code>@langchain/core</code> 1.0.x</li>
<li>Your project has <code>@langchain/core</code> 0.3.x</li>
</ul>
<p>Fix your imports references to import from <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@langchain/classic#when-not-to-use-this-package" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>langchain</code> package vs legacy imports from <code>@langchain/classic/</code></a></p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>import { ChatOllama, OllamaEmbeddings } from &quot;@langchain/ollama&quot;;
import { ChatPromptTemplate, MessagesPlaceholder } from &quot;@langchain/core/prompts&quot;;

import { createStuffDocumentsChain } from &quot;langchain/chains/combine_documents&quot;;

import { CheerioWebBaseLoader } from &quot;@langchain/community/document_loaders/web/cheerio&quot;;

import { RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter } from &quot;@langchain/textsplitters&quot;;

import { MemoryVectorStore } from &quot;langchain/vectorstores/memory&quot;;
import { createRetrievalChain } from &quot;langchain/chains/retrieval&quot;;
import { createHistoryAwareRetriever } from &quot;langchain/chains/history_aware_retriever&quot;;

import { AIMessage, HumanMessage } from &quot;@langchain/core/messages&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>If using <code>@langchain/core</code> 1.1.x, you can keep your current import references as they are. <a href="https://github.com/osule/experiment/commit/a79b01d2c512a3b8f5bc5cad625f059cff1ad976" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Refer to reference project</a></p>
<p>Use the following compatible versions in your <code>package.json</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
    &quot;@langchain/classic&quot;: &quot;^1.0.15&quot;,
    &quot;@langchain/community&quot;: &quot;^1.1.12&quot;,
    &quot;@langchain/core&quot;: &quot;^1.1.19&quot;,
    &quot;@langchain/ollama&quot;: &quot;^1.2.2&quot;,
    &quot;cheerio&quot;: &quot;^1.2.0&quot;,
    &quot;langchain&quot;: &quot;^1.2.18&quot;
  },
</code></pre>
<p>You may need to <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchainjs/tree/langchain%401.2.18/libs/langchain-community#quick-install" rel="nofollow noreferrer">hint your package manager that it should use your specific version of <code>@langchain/core</code></a> and not regard what version <code>@langchain/community</code> depends on.</p>
<p>For npm, in your <code>package.json</code> add:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>  &quot;overrides&quot;: {
    &quot;@langchain/core&quot;: &quot;^1.1.19&quot;
  },
</code></pre>
<p>For yarn, in your <code>package.json</code> add:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>  &quot;resolutions&quot;: {
    &quot;@langchain/core&quot;: &quot;^1.1.19&quot;
  },
</code></pre>
<p>For pnpm, in your <code>package.json</code> add:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>  &quot;pnpm&quot;: {
    &quot;overrides&quot;: {
      &quot;@langchain/core&quot;: &quot;^1.1.19&quot;
    },
  }
</code></pre>

Q: OrderBy pipe in angular 4 A: <p>It will work even for <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code> <code>''</code> and any type of data type like number string bool:</p>
<pre><code>transform(arr: any[], property: any, direction: number): any[] | null {

    if(!arr || arr?.length == 0)
      return arr;

      arr.sort((a: any, b: any) =&gt; {
      let v1 = a[property]?? '';
      let v2 = b[property]?? '';
      if(v1 &lt; v2)
        return 1 * direction;
      else if(v1 &gt; v2)
        return -1 * direction;
      else 
        return 0 * direction;
    });
    return arr;
  }
</code></pre>

Q: Add columns to table in Javascript A: <p>Here's a function to add a column to a table with pure javascript:</p>
<pre><code>function addColumnToTableJS(table, pos, content){
  if (typeof table === 'string'){
      table = document.getElementById(table);
  }
  var columns = table.rows[0].getElementsByTagName('td').length;
  if (!pos &amp;&amp; pos !== 0){
    pos = columns;
  }
  var rowregex = new RegExp('{ROWINDEX}', 'g');
  var colregex = new RegExp('{COLINDEX}', 'g');
  for (var r=0; r&lt;table.rows.length; r++){
    var cell = table.rows[r].insertCell(pos);
    cell.innerHTML = content
      .replace(rowregex, String.fromCharCode(65 + r))
      .replace(colregex, columns + 1); 
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In its simplest usage, call like this to add column to the left:</p>
<pre><code>addColumnToTableJS('table1', 0, '&lt;td&gt;test&lt;/td&gt;')
</code></pre>
<p>Or like this to add the column to the right:</p>
<pre><code>addColumnToTableJS('table1', null, '&lt;td&gt;test&lt;/td&gt;')
</code></pre>
<p>Here's an example:</p>
